*** Add File: modules/09-reusable-architecture/README.md
# Module 09: Reusable Test Architecture

Move from one-off scripts to maintainable suites. You will design page objects, component objects, typed test data, custom fixtures, and role-specific browser contexts without hiding important Playwright behavior.

This is the point where abstraction becomes useful: extract repeated business actions, not every locator. A good page object exposes user intent such as `orders.filter_by_status("paid")`; it does not turn every Playwright method into another wrapper.

Read Objectives → Concepts → Exercises → Verification. The local commerce portal supplies deterministic data.
*** Add File: modules/09-reusable-architecture/01-objectives/README.md
# Objectives

By the end of this module you can:

- model a page and a repeated UI component with small Python classes;
- create custom pytest fixtures with explicit scope and teardown;
- represent test data with typed dataclasses;
- create separate authenticated contexts for different user roles;
- identify when an abstraction reduces duplication and when it merely hides Playwright.
*** Add File: modules/09-reusable-architecture/02-concepts/README.md
# Concepts: Reusable Architecture

## Page objects

A page object owns selectors and user-level operations for one page. Tests should read like a behavior specification while the page object absorbs selector maintenance. Keep assertions that describe business outcomes in the test; keep interaction mechanics in the object.

## Component objects

Repeated UI such as a table, date picker, or navigation bar is a component, not a page. Compose a page from components instead of creating a deep inheritance tree.

## Fixture scope

Function scope gives isolation; session scope is appropriate for an immutable server or browser binary, not for a mutable logged-in page. A fixture that yields an object must clean it up after the yield.

## Roles and contexts

A browser context is the boundary for cookies and local storage. Create one context per role or scenario when comparing permissions. Never switch a single page between users in one test.
*** Add File: modules/09-reusable-architecture/03-exercises/README.md
# Exercises

Create an `OrdersPage` with `login()` and `filter_by_status()` methods, an `OrderRow` component, and a typed order record. Add a fixture that produces a fresh page object per test. Then write two tests that use separate contexts for signed-out and signed-in behavior.
